shortAnswer: "Check the things AI is specifically unreliable about, rather than re-reading everything. In practice that means: verify every number, date and name; check anything cited as fact, law or policy; confirm it did what you asked rather than something adjacent; and read the opening and closing lines properly, since those carry the most weight with the reader."
faq:
  - q: "Doesn't reviewing cancel out the time saved?"
    a: "Only if you re-do the work. Reviewing to a checklist takes a fraction of the time writing does. The trap is open-ended re-reading — knowing what you're looking for is what keeps the saving."
  - q: "How do I spot a made-up fact?"
    a: "Specificity with no source is the tell. A precise-sounding statistic, a named regulation, a court case, a person's exact title — these are where fabrications cluster, because a plausible-looking specific is easy to generate. If it matters, verify it independently."
  - q: "Is AI output more or less reliable than a junior staff member?"
    a: "Different, not simply better or worse. A junior member says when they're unsure; AI states a wrong answer in the same confident register as a right one. That's the whole reason review has to be systematic rather than instinctive."
  - q: "What can we skip reviewing?"
    a: "Low-stakes internal material — a rough summary, brainstorming, a first draft you'll rewrite anyway. Reserve the effort for anything that leaves the business, gets relied on, or can't be undone."
  - q: "Should we tell clients when AI was involved?"
    a: "There's no general obligation for ordinary drafting where a person reviews and takes responsibility. It matters more in regulated or advice-giving contexts — worth agreeing a position rather than leaving it to individuals."
